  nonce: a83f  # a83f
  data:  # a83f
    kind: invariant  # a83f
    title: "bash calls a name assigned only in the SOURCING script an environment read, so a sourced variable becomes a host_secret source"  # a83f
    status: violated  # a83f
    priority: 2  # a83f
    parent:  # a83f
    tags:  # a83f
    - taint  # a83f
    - bash  # a83f
    - dataflow  # a83f
    isbefore: []  # a83f
    duplicate_of: []  # a83f
    not_duplicate_of: []  # a83f
    pr_ref:  # a83f
    description: "FOUND BY CLOSING INV-fumod SHAPE (b), AND IT IS THE ROW THAT SURVIVED IT.\nFiled separately on the owner's ruling rather than folded in, because it is a\ndifferent mechanism with a different fix.\n\nTHE DEFECT. bash.py's env-read rule is \"an expansion whose name is never\nassigned in THIS FILE came from the environment\" -- the conservative\ndiscriminator available without cross-file analysis. It is wrong whenever a\nscript is `source`d by another that assigns the name. Live instance, cold,\nafter shape (b) closed:\n\n  guacamole-docker/build.d/020-download-drivers.sh\n    :59  local DEST_PATH=\"$DESTINATION/drivers/\"\n    :71  curl -L \"$URL\" > \"$DEST_PATH/$DEST_JAR\"\n\n  reports host_secret -> host_fs, sourced at DESTINATION. DESTINATION is NOT\n  an environment variable: it is assigned exactly once in the whole tree, at\n  guacamole-docker/bin/build-guacamole.sh:58 (DESTINATION=\"$2\"), supplied as\n  the literal /opt/guacamole at Dockerfile:72, and 020-download-drivers.sh runs\n  only because build-guacamole.sh:61-63 SOURCES it. Measurement 0006's human\n  refuter established the same thing by hand (\"$DESTINATION is NOT environ ...\n  grep -rn 'export ' guacamole-docker/ returns only ...\").\n\nWHY IT IS NOT INV-fumod SHAPE (b). That shape is \"bytes fetched by a command\nwhose ARGUMENT is tainted are credited to the redirect\" -- a claim about the\nBYTES, now refused (the MYSQL_JDBC_VERSION row is gone; violated(6) ->\nviolated(3) on the item's own instance). This row is credited through the\nredirect's OPERAND by a name that should never have been a taint source at\nall. Different half of the sink, different cause, different fix.\n\nTHE FIX IS CROSS-`source` NAME REACH, and the graph already exists: bash.py\nemits source/. edges (its `source`/`.` branch), and WI-zovuz's closure is\nwhole-file. What is missing is following an import edge so a name assigned in\nthe SOURCING script is known to be assigned. Note the ordering hazard: bash is\ndynamically scoped and a sourced script sees the sourcer's assignments, so the\nclosure must run over the source-graph, not per file in isolation.\n\nSCOPE NOTE, so this is not read as bigger than it is: WI-zovuz already\nestablished the machinery (derivation closure, positional binding, fail-closed\non parse errors, the `<key>_values` collapsed-edge reader). This is that\nclosure taught to cross one edge type.\n\nNOT MEASURED YET: how many cohort findings rest on a name assigned only in a\nsourcing script. The guacamole row is one. Measuring that population is step\none, before any gate -- the same order that saved WI-zovuz from shipping a\nbyte-producer rule that deleted a real signing certificate."  # a83f
    fields:  # a83f
      statement: "A bash expansion whose name is never assigned in its own file is classified as an environment read, so a variable assigned in a script that SOURCES it (guacamole's DESTINATION, assigned at build-guacamole.sh:58 as $2) becomes a host_secret taint source and carries a host_fs finding through the redirect's target operand."  # a83f
      root_cause: "The env-read discriminator is scoped to a single file because no cross-file name analysis exists; bash.py emits source/. edges but the WI-zovuz derivation closure does not follow them."  # a83f
- op: update  # ab62
  at: '2026-09-01T00:09:08Z'  # ab62
  by: agent  # ab62
  actor: jgstern_agent  # ab62
  clock: 2  # ab62
  nonce: ab62  # ab62
  set:  # ab62
    fields:  # ab62
      root_cause: "The env-read discriminator is scoped to a single file because no cross-file name analysis exists. CORRECTED 2026-08-31: the original text said the fix is that \"bash.py emits source/. edges but the WI-zovuz derivation closure does not follow them\". Following those edges does NOT reach this item's own instance. guacamole sources via `for SCRIPT in /opt/guacamole/build.d/*.sh; do source \"$SCRIPT\"; done` (build-guacamole.sh:61-63), so the emitted `sources` edge carries sourced_path=\"$SCRIPT\" -- a loop variable over a glob of a CONTAINER path that does not exist in the repository tree. The files live at guacamole-docker/build.d/ and are connected to /opt/guacamole/build.d/ only by Dockerfile:64 COPY. Reaching the instance through the edge therefore needs variable resolution AND glob expansion AND a cross-language Dockerfile path mapping."  # ab62
- op: discuss  # b08e
  at: '2026-09-01T00:14:31Z'  # b08e
  by: agent  # b08e
  actor: jgstern_agent  # b08e
  clock: 3  # b08e
  nonce: b08e  # b08e
  message: "STEP ONE DONE — POPULATION MEASURED, AND THE FIX AS FILED IS REFUTED. Artifact:\n~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/pujob_bash_cross_source_08312026/ (README first).\nCohort = measurement 0006's 16 repos. Status stays violated: the DEFECT\nreproduces, only the stated remedy is wrong.\n\nWHY THE STATED FIX CANNOT WORK — the root_cause field is corrected in place.\nIt said \"bash.py emits source/. edges but the closure does not follow them\",\nwhich implies following them suffices. Measured over all 97 source/. statements\nin the cohort, taking each argument as bash.py's edge does:\n\n    variable_or_cmdsub      80  (82.5%)   <- no followable target\n    resolves_relative       12  (12.4%)\n    absolute_path_missing    4  ( 4.1%)   <- container paths\n    basename_match           1  ( 1.0%)\n\nOnly 12 of 97 have a target the edge can follow, and NONE of them is in\nguacamole. This item's own instance is\n`for SCRIPT in /opt/guacamole/build.d/*.sh; do source \"$SCRIPT\"; done`\n(build-guacamole.sh:61-63): a loop variable over a glob of a CONTAINER path.\nThe scripts live at guacamole-docker/build.d/ and are joined to\n/opt/guacamole/build.d/ only by Dockerfile:64 COPY. Reaching the filed row\nthrough the edge needs variable resolution AND glob expansion AND a\ncross-language Dockerfile mapping.\n\nTHE BLUNT ALTERNATIVE IS ALSO REFUTED, at the construct level by read-back and\nat the finding level by arithmetic. \"A name assigned in ANY bash file of the\nrepo is not an environment read\" would touch 210 of 545 (file,name) env-read\npairs (38.5%). Read back:\n\n  * guacamole 10 of 10 hits are GENUINE sourced variables (DESTINATION,\n    BUILD_DIR, GUACAMOLE_HOME, VAR_PREFIX, CATALINA_BASE, LEGACY_VAR_NAME).\n  * rabbitmq refutes the rule with two rows: selenium/authorization-server/mvnw\n    (a VENDORED maven wrapper, sourced by nothing) reads $HOME, matched against\n    an unrelated setup-erlang.sh -- a REAL env read the rule would delete; and\n    the generic names `key`, `value`, `opt` collide across unrelated trees.\n\nAnd at the FINDING level it buys nothing at all. The cohort has 32 bash-sourced\nevidence rows, every one env_read. Rows where ALL of the source file's env names\nare assigned elsewhere in the repo: ZERO. 16 SOME, 16 NONE. Same shape as\nWI-zovuz's own lesson -- 38.5% at the construct level, 0% at the finding level.\n\nWHAT THE MEASUREMENT SAYS THE REAL FIX IS, and it is tractable. The dynamic\ntargets are dynamic in the PREFIX and literal in the TAIL:\n\n    source $TOOL_LIB_PATH/gitlib.sh\n    source $(dirname $(readlink -ne $BASH_SOURCE))/k8s-common.sh\n\nResolving a target by its literal trailing component against repo bash files:\n\n    static (fully literal)              17  (17.5%)\n    dynamic, literal tail, UNIQUE match 62  (63.9%)   <- recovered\n    dynamic, no literal tail            13  (13.4%)\n    dynamic, literal tail not in repo    5  ( 5.2%)\n    AMBIGUOUS (tail matches 2+ files)    0\n\n81 of 97 (83.5%) become followable, up from 12. Zero ambiguity on this cohort.\nThe residual 13 are bare `$SCRIPT` / `$ENV_FILE` / `$f`; guacamole's 2 are the\nglob-loop shape and need the loop binding plus a directory-suffix match\n(`/opt/guacamole/build.d/` -> the repo's unique `build.d/`).\n\nVERIFIED THE PATTERN GENERALISES beyond guacamole. cilium contrib/backporting:\ncheck-stable sources k8s-common.sh, gitlib.sh and common.sh by this exact\ndynamic-prefix/literal-tail shape; TOOL_LIB_PATH is assigned in k8s-common.sh\nand GHCURL / CILIUM_GITHUB_API in gitlib.sh, so all three are genuine sourced\nvariables today counted as environment reads. PROG is the REVERSE direction --\nassigned in check-stable:19 and read in the file check-stable SOURCES -- which\nis guacamole's shape, so propagation must run BOTH ways along the edge (bash is\ndynamically scoped).\n\nDESIGN CONSTRAINT, non-negotiable: this REMOVES taint sources, the\nfalse-all-clear direction. Resolution must FAIL CLOSED -- an unresolved source\ntarget adds no assignments and the name stays an environment read.\n\nREMAINING UNKNOWN, stated rather than papered over: I have NOT measured how many\nof the 32 bash rows a perfect cross-source analysis actually deletes. That needs\nper-row REACHING names (the intersection WI-zovuz's _source_names_can_reach_sink\ncomputes), not the per-file env sets used above, and a row only dies if EVERY\nname reaching its sink turns out to be sourced. The affected subset is at most\nthe 16 SOME rows; the true figure is somewhere in 0..16 and I will not guess it."  # b08e
